            0.232,
            0.247,
            0.845,
            0.309
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "An example of a formula — Area of a rectangle = length × width. ["
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hint:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " There is a relation between the areas of EFGH, the path, and ABCD.]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.313,
            0.847,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) If the width of the path along each side is given, can you find its area? If not, what other measurements do you need? Assign values of your choice to these measurements and find the area of the path. Give a formula for the area using these measurements. ["
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hint:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Break the path into rectangles.]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.19,
            0.416,
            0.846,
            0.473
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) Does the area of the path change when the outer rectangle is moved while keeping the inner rectangular park EFGH inside it, as shown?"
